  "body": "The spec references the \"origin\" of a Document quite often, but it never points to a specific \"origin\" member associated with a Document. Instead it always just points to the HTML Standard definition of an \"origin\". Here are some examples:\r\n - https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#resource-metadata-management:concept-origin\r\n - https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#navigating-across-documents:concept-origin-2\r\n\r\nThis contrasts with how we treat other Document-associated data such as [referrer](referrer), [referrer policy](rp) and [URL][url]. For these bits we link directly to the definition of this member that is explicitly associated with a given Document.\r\n\r\nGiven this implicitness, I was assuming that this \"origin\" of a Document was simply the origin of its URL member, however as @Kaiido and I discovered in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/6243, a Document's URL can be `about:blank`, while its origin is not the unique opaque origin associated with that URL.\r\n\r\nShould we formalize the \"origin\" member of a Document, and make its relationship with its URL clear? Am I overlooking something huge here and making this more complex than it needs to be?\r\n\r\n[referrer]: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#navigating-across-documents:the-document's-referrer-2\r\n[rp]: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#navigating-across-documents:concept-document-referrer-policy\r\n[url]: https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-url",
